! mcp_bootstrap_p152.strap -- the tail of the disk arc-address
! computation (Z DISK 1) and the whole of the CHANGE MODE routine
! (K MODE), printed page 152.  docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.159,
! header READ and not assumed: the top line prints "TIME CLOCK
! 00054041  PUNID 11D11MCP   CARD ID IA000047   DIST. OF 3/01
! 3/01   PAGE NUMBER  152", and the column-heading row's own
! "LOCATION 217573" names the page's first word.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FIFTY-ONE PRINTED LINES, forty-two of which emit bits.  The page
! prints NO SLC and NO DD/SYN/VF: every emitting row is an
! instruction except the last three, which are index words (XW).
! Lines 13 and 28-34 are COMMENT CARDS -- a "-" in the NAME column,
! no LOCATION, no BINARY OUTPUT -- and line 41 is a CNOP that found
! the counter already on a word boundary and so printed NEITHER a
! location NOR any bits (printed p.151 line 46's CNOP, two rows
! earlier in the listing, DID emit "000000.30 00"; the difference is
! the alignment it arrives on, and this page's line 40 is a full
! word ending exactly at 217621.00).
!
! Two routines, back to back:
!
!   lines 1-27   the tail of the DISK request path.  Line 7's label
!                Z DISK 1 is the entry: it tests S LOC SU and
!                S HS ECH, checks the channel is not already
!                operating (BBZ -> K C BUSY), pulls the control
!                word's count field out of S CW, tests for a
!                complete arc, decrements by one for a full-arc
!                request, adds the current arc and compares against
!                the file's maximum (BXH -> K ILEGL, "-TOO MANY
!                ARCS"), then loads the arc address, tests whether a
!                LOC is needed, and leaves either through K G LOC
!                (with the IC saved in K G LOCR) or through
!                Z STEVL.  Lines 1-6 are the tail of whatever
!                precedes it on printed p.151: they set the RD
!                operation bit, test an index, set the HS ECH bit
!                and go to M IOREJ by way of K SUPP.
!
!   lines 29-51  "*****CHANGE MODE*****", entered at K MODE (line
!                35, 217615.00).  Its own comment cards give the
!                calling sequence -- "LV,1,(CODE)" then
!                "SIC,K K MODE;B,K MODE" -- and the two-bit code:
!                01 ECC odd, 10 no-ECC even, 11 no-ECC odd.  It
!                compares the wanted mode against the channel
!                status table (KF), returns at once if it already
!                matches (BAE), otherwise picks the octal code out
!                of the K M CODE table, plants it in the mode-change
!                command at K MODE C, resets the table (SF), saves
!                $0 into S IO INS, issues the CTL(SEOP), and waits
!                for end-op through K SEOP T / K EOP R / M IO REJ.
!                K K MODE (line 48) is the "B,$" the caller's SIC
!                modifies.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FORTY-SIX CARDS OVER FIFTY-ONE LINES, AND NOTHING FELL OFF EITHER
! END
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The card-serial column runs IA000047 (line 1) to IA000092 (line
! 51), read at 400 dpi off the right-hand band.  Line 1's serial is
! also the header's own CARD ID, so nothing fell off the top of the
! render; line 51 is followed by blank paper to the foot of the
! form, and printed p.153 opens with its own asterisk box, so
! nothing fell off the bottom.  The serials are consecutive, with
! FIVE printed twice -- one card, two statements, exactly the shape
! printed p.136 line 1-2 and printed p.127 line 56 carry:
!
!   IA000048  lines 2,3    KVI,4,.32          ; BZXE,$+1.32
!   IA000050  lines 5,6    LVI,1,K SUPP       ; B,M IOREJ
!   IA000069  lines 25,26  SIC,K G LOCR       ; B,K G LOC
!   IA000085  lines 42,43  LX,0,$+1.          ; SX,0,S IO INS($12)
!   IA000088  lines 46,47  SIC,K EOP R        ; B,M IO REJ
!
! 51 lines - 5 doubled = 46 cards = IA000092 - IA000047 + 1.  The
! two SIC pairs are on this page in full, so the straddle of
! docs/transcription_workflow.md's "A SIC/BRANCH PAIR CAN STRADDLE A
! PAGE BREAK" does not arise here and no row is left out.
!
! >>> THE SURVEY IS WRONG ABOUT THIS PAGE AND THE CARD COLUMN SAYS
! SO. <<<  strap/mcp_survey.py read 29 rows, 217574.00..217621.00.
! The truth is 42 emitting rows, 217573.00..217627.00: it lost line
! 1 off the top (the page's FIRST row, and the one the heading
! LOCATION field names) and lines 42-51 off the bottom, including
! all three XW index words.  Counted off the card serials, as the
! brief requires.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOINS -- BOTH SIDES CLOSED, NEITHER BY THIS PAGE'S INK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THE PAGE PRINTS NO SLC, SO NO !CHECK BELOW CAN TEST THE
! ORIGIN. <<<  A hard SLC overrides the arriving counter silently
! (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "A HARD SLC CANNOT DISAGREE WITH
! THE ARRIVING COUNTER"), so every !CHECK here would still pass if
! 217573.00 were wrong.  It is nevertheless fixed four ways, three
! of which never touch this page:
!
!   (a) THE HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION 217573", printed by IBM's
!       assembler at the right-hand end of the column-heading line
!       and read, not assumed.  It gives the word and not the bit.
!   (b) NEAR SIDE.  The volume OCR of PDF p.158 -- printed p.151, a
!       DIFFERENT page and therefore an admissible witness -- ends
!       at its line 48: "48* 217572.00 * 000000.00 8E 000000.01 00
!       K GO  RD,0.0($14),0  -ISSUE RD COMMAND".  That is a full
!       word at 217572.00, so it occupies 217572.00 and 217572.40
!       and leaves the counter at exactly 217573.00.
!   (c) FAR SIDE.  The volume OCR of PDF p.160 -- printed p.153 --
!       carries, after its three-line asterisk box, an EMITTING row
!       at 217630.00 ("217630.00  221140.00 80", a SIC), which is
!       where p.153's own code begins.  Line 51 here is a full word
!       at 217627.00 and so leaves the counter at exactly
!       217630.00.  They agree, and the agreement does not depend
!       on reading the garbled line beside it -- the OCR ALSO shows
!       a second, NON-emitting row printed at 217630.00 just above,
!       which is the shape of a printed "SLC,$"; if that is what it
!       is, it is the free forward-join witness of
!       docs/transcription_workflow.md, but the join is closed
!       without it.
!   (d) TWO OUTSIDE PAGES PIN TWO OF THIS PAGE'S OWN LABELS, and
!       between them they pin the origin to the BIT:
!         Z DISK 1 at 217577.00 -- printed p.151 line 14 prints
!           "217551.00  217577.10 00  B,Z DISK 1  -DISK - 1" and
!           printed p.153 line 14 prints "217635.00  217577.10 00
!           B,Z DISK1  -DISK".  A plain B prints target+.10, so both
!           put Z DISK 1 on the EVEN half word of 217577.
!         K K MODE at 217624.40 -- printed p.152's own neighbours
!           print "SIC,K K MODE" against "217624.40 80" TWICE on
!           other pages (OCR lines 8006 and 9434), and SIC prints
!           the BARE 24-bit address with no operation bits in the
!           offset field (measured on printed p.143 line 8), so
!           K K MODE is pinned to the BIT, not the half word.
!         K MODE at 217615.00 -- three other pages print
!           "B,K MODE" against "217615.10 00" (OCR 7882, 8008,
!           9435).
!       This deck's counter reaches 217577.00 after six rows,
!       217615.00 after twenty-six emitting rows and 217624.40
!       after thirty-eight.  A wrong origin moves all three
!       together and all three are independently printed elsewhere.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS -- WHAT WAS SETTLED AND ON WHAT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page opens round glyphs constantly: its own column heading
! prints "LOCATICN" and "BINARY CUTPUT", and LOCATIONS print
! "217573.CC" for .00.  A round glyph in a LOCATION or in an OCTAL
! BINARY field is a 0 with certainty (C is not an octal digit).
! The glyphs that are NOT in an octal field were each settled on
! something else, and on this page almost all of them are settled
! by the ASSEMBLER, because the field they sit in is checked:
!
! (1) "CTO111" IS "CT0111", AND THE BITS SAY SO, NOT THE INK.  The
!     connective's four binary digits land in the second half word's
!     offset field, where the oracle sees them.  Measured, varying
!     nothing else (line 12, printed 011062.57 70):
!         CT0111(BU,9),S CW+.37($13),-27   011062.57 70  = PRINTED
!         CT0000(...)                      011062.41 70
!         CT1111(...)                      011062.77 70
!         C0111(...)                       011062.56 70
!         CM0111(...)                      011062.56 F0
!     Only CT0111 matches.  The round glyph after "CT" cannot be an
!     O in any case -- a connective's digits are binary -- but the
!     measurement also settles CT against C and CM, which ink could
!     not.
!
! (2) THE TRAILING BYTE ON LINE 23 IS "0C", NOT "00" OR "CC".  It
!     prints as two round glyphs.  A branch-on-bit's trailing byte
!     is bits 56-63: the top nibble is part of the literal 111000000
!     and is always 0, and the bottom nibble is mod+cond.  Measured:
!         BZB1,SLOCSU,KKLOC   220065.74 0C  = PRINTED
!         BZB,...             220065.74 00
!         BZBZ,...            220065.74 04
!         BZBN,...            220065.74 08
!         BB1,...             220065.74 0E
!     So the first glyph is a damaged 0 and the second is a real C,
!     IN THE SAME TWO-CHARACTER FIELD -- the sharpest illustration
!     on this page of "ink varies per CHARACTER, not per row".  The
!     same measurement is what spells the mnemonic BZB1 (and lines
!     7, 8, 9 and 24 are BB, BZB, BBZ and BZBZ by the same check).
!
! (3) THE TRAILING BYTE ON LINE 14 IS "C0", AND HERE THE C IS REAL.
!     Read "00" at 400 dpi; the assembler said C0; rendered at 1800
!     dpi the first glyph is open on the right with no top or bottom
!     closure (a C) and the second is a closed ring (a 0), and the
!     row below prints "0D" with the opposite shapes for comparison.
!     The bits agree:  BZRZ,$+1.0 -> 217605.74 C0, BRZ -> ...74 C2.
!     An indicator branch's byte carries the indicator; C0/C2 differ
!     only in the "branch on" bit, which is what the leading Z in
!     BZRZ means.  >>> THIS IS THE ONE PLACE A FIRST READING OF THIS
!     PAGE WAS WRONG, AND WHAT CAUGHT IT WAS THE ASSEMBLER
!     DISAGREEING, NOT A SECOND LOOK. <<<
!
! (4) "SF" IS NOT "ST", AND "KF" IS NOT "K".  Lines 36 and 40 are
!     the same operand with different operations.  Measured:
!         KF(BU,2),S C MODE($10),-18   002067.23 10  = PRINTED
!         K(BU,2),...                  002067.21 10
!         KFE(BU,2),...                002067.23 30
!         KR(BU,2),...                 002067.21 50
!         SF(BU,2),S C MODE($10),-18   002067.12 F0  = PRINTED
!         ST(BU,2),...                 002067.20 D0
!
! (5) "CTL(SEOP)" IS CHECKED AGAINST ITS WHOLE FAMILY (line 44):
!         CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),0.0   000000.15 00  = PRINTED
!         CTL,...                  000000.05 00
!         RD(SEOP),...             000000.11 00
!         W(SEOP),...              000000.13 00
!         LOC(SEOP),...            000000.17 00
!     and the index "($14)" is checked by the first half word's byte
!     (8E; "($4)" prints 84).
!
! (6) INDEX NUMBERS ARE DECIMAL AND THE PAGE PROVES IT TO ITSELF.
!     The low nibble of a VFL full word's first byte is i1, so
!     ($10)->A, ($11)->B, ($12)->C, ($13)->D, ($14)->E.  Lines 1
!     (8B), 9 (8A), 10 (8D), 20 (8A), 24 (8B), 36 (8A), 43 (1C) and
!     44 (8E) print exactly that, which is five different index
!     registers agreeing across eight rows.
!
! (7) "K EOP R" AND "K SEOP T" END AND BEGIN IN O's, NOT C's.  The
!     volume's own table of contents prints the routine's title --
!     "EOP TEST FOR SEOP TYPE I/O   PAGE 0187" -- and line 47's
!     comment column is the English word: "-WAIT FOR END OP".  EOP
!     is also an indicator name in the operation set (C22-6531).
!
! (8) THE "1" IN "Z DISK 1" IS A DIGIT.  Rendered at 1800 dpi the
!     final glyph has the drum's digit-1 shape -- an upper-left flag
!     stroke into a bare stem with a base serif and NO top bar --
!     against the full top-and-bottom serifs of the I two characters
!     to its left in the same name.  Independently, printed p.151
!     line 14 prints the reference as "B,Z DISK 1" with the comment
!     "-DISK - 1", and printed p.153 line 14 prints "B,Z DISK1".
!
! (9) >>> "K MODE C" AND "K M CODE" REST ON INK AND NOTHING ELSE,
!     SO THEY WERE READ AT 1200 dpi AGAINST A CALIBRATION INSIDE
!     THEIR OWN WORDS. <<<  They are the page's only two labels
!     that nothing outside this page references (Z DISK 1, K MODE
!     and K K MODE are all pinned by other pages; see THE JOINS),
!     so listing_oracle and check_symbol_spellings are both blind
!     to them -- exactly the class that "assembles perfectly and is
!     simply wrong".  What settles them:
!       * Line 49's name prints "CODE" with the C and the O
!         ADJACENT: the C is open on the right with flared
!         terminals, the O is a closed ring, in one word off one
!         drum one character apart.  Line 44's name prints the same
!         two shapes a word apart -- a closed O in "MODE" and an
!         open C as the trailing character -- and the D of both
!         shows the straight vertical left stem and squared corners
!         that distinguish it from C and 0 (the discriminator of
!         docs/transcription_workflow.md's "C vs D").
!       * Line 35's "K MODE" is pinned from outside (three pages
!         print "B,K MODE" at 217615.10), so its MODE is a
!         known-good instance of the same string in the same
!         column, and lines 44 and 48 print it identically.
!       * The comment column is English and says both words: line
!         36 "-IS CHAN SET TO DESIRED MODE", line 38 "-IF NOT,
!         PICK UP OCTAL CODE", line 44 "-MODE CHANGE COMMAND"
!         (which is what the trailing C of K MODE C abbreviates),
!         and the routine's own comment cards on lines 31 and 33
!         print "LV,1,(CODE)" and "***  CODE  **".
!
! (10) THE SAME SYMBOL IS PRINTED WITH AND WITHOUT ITS BLANKS ON THIS
!     ONE PAGE, which is the assembler's own witness that the blanks
!     are not part of the name: line 4 prints "S HS ECH" and line 8
!     prints "SHSECH"; line 6 prints "M IOREJ" and line 47 prints
!     "M IO REJ".  Both are written closed up below, as every deck
!     since p.2 does, and all are within the six-character limit
!     (C22-6531 printed p.10).
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SECOND ASTERISK, AND WHAT THIS PAGE ADDS TO IT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every one of the fifty-one line numbers carries the ordinary
! asterisk ("1*", "51*").  TWO lines carry a SECOND asterisk after
! the LOCATION: line 17 (217606.00) and line 43 (217621.40).  That
! is the unexplained marker of docs/listing_typography.md 12.
! What this page contributes:
!   * both marked rows are HALF-WORD DIRECT INDEX instructions
!     (KV and SX) -- so "a full word on an odd half word", which
!     printed p.144 already disconfirmed, stays dead;
!   * one is on an even half word and one on an odd one, so
!     alignment is not it;
!   * line 43 is the SECOND statement of card IA000085 and line 17
!     is the only statement on card IA000061, so "the second
!     statement punched on one card" is not it either (which
!     printed p.3 had already shown);
!   * the pitch here is 26 lines on a 51-line page, against printed
!     p.3's 22-23 on 52 lines and printed p.144's 16 on 30 lines.
!     That is ordered the same way as the "fixed physical distance
!     down the form" reading in 12, but p.3 and this page are the
!     same density and 22-23 is not 26, so it does not settle it.
! Recorded as open, with nothing added to it.  It changes no bit:
! both rows are in the checked set below and both match.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- every one defined by a card on a DIFFERENT page
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ZERO TAUTOLOGICAL ROWS.  This page's own cards sit at OCR lines
! ~7891-7946 of docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt; every witness cited
! below is a different printed page, so none of them is this page
! reading itself.  Twelve of the twenty-two are already carried by
! LANDED decks at these same values.
!
! S COM RG 215001.00  printed p.103 line 8 DEFINES it, "S COM RG
!                     XW,(8)520000."  Carried only so S HS ECH and
!                     S LOC SU can be written the way p.103 writes
!                     them.
SCOMRG  SYN, (8)215001.0
! S HS ECH 215001.01  p.103 line 11 DEFINES it, "S HS ECH
!                     SYN(BU,1),S COM RG+.1" -HS ECH OPERATION BIT.
!                     Lines 4 and 8 here print 215001.01 as a full
!                     24-bit BIT address, so they pin it to the bit.
SHSECH  SYN(BU,1), SCOMRG+.1
! S LOC SU 215001.21  p.103 line 26 DEFINES it, "S LOC SU
!                     SYN(BU,1),S COM RG.17" -ON WHEN DISK IN SETUP.
!                     Lines 7 and 23 print the bit address in full.
SLOCSU  SYN(BU,1), SCOMRG.17
! S RD     000000.71  printed p.99 line 45 "-READ, OPERATION STATUS";
!                     carried by five landed decks as SYN(BU,1),0.57
!                     (57 decimal = 0o71).  Line 1 pins it to the bit.
SRD     SYN(BU,1), 0.57
! S CH OP  000000.32  printed p.99 "-CHAN OPERATING"; four landed
!                     decks carry 0.26 (= 0o32).  Line 9 pins the bit.
SCHOP   SYN(BU,1), 0.26
! S SEL    000000.47  printed p.99 line 32 "-SELECT FILE STATUS";
!                     four landed decks carry 0.39 (= 0o47).  Line 24
!                     pins the bit.
SSEL    SYN(BU,1), 0.39
! S C MODE 000000.62  printed p.99 line 37 "-CHANNEL MODE", 0.50
!                     (= 0o62).  Lines 36 and 40 pin the bit.
SCMODE  SYN(BU,2), 0.50
! S ARCAD  000000.40  printed p.99 line 52 "-ARC ADDRESS (DISK)",
!                     0.32 (= 0o40).  Line 20 pins the bit.
SARCAD  SYN(BU,18), 0.32
! S CW     000003.00  printed p.102 line 14 "-CONTROL WORD";
!                     decks p.102, p.129 and p.138 carry 3.0.  Lines
!                     10 and 12 pin it to the bit, through two
!                     DIFFERENT offsets (.28 and +.37 decimal, which
!                     print .34 and .45 octal).
SCW     SYN(BU,64), 3.0
! S DD     000000.00  printed p.108's defining card; decks p.108 and
!                     p.129 carry SYN(BU,7),0.  Line 10 writes its
!                     byte size as "9-S DD" and the byte-size field
!                     is SEVEN BITS, so this page pins S DD only
!                     MOD 128 -- see WHAT THE CHECKS DO NOT SHOW.
SDD     SYN(BU,7), 0
! S CU ARC 000006.00  printed p.102 line 19 "-CURRENT ARC"; deck
!                     p.129 carries 6.00 too.  Lines 16 and 19 pin
!                     it only to the half word (measured below).
SCUARC  SYN(BU,18), 6.00
! S MX ARC 000005.40  printed p.102 line 18 "- MAXIMUM ARC", 5.32
!                     (= 0o40).  Line 17 pins the half word.
SMXARC  SYN(BU,18), 5.32
! S IO INS 000004.00  printed p.101 line 17 "-IO INSTR. LAST"; decks
!                     p.101, p.122 and p.135 carry 4.0.  Line 43
!                     pins the half word.
SIOINS  SYN(BU,64), 4.0
! K SUPP   216242.00  printed p.128 line 3 is the label row,
!                     "K SUPP  NOP,K COMM R"; eleven landed decks
!                     carry it.  Line 5 is an LVI and pins only the
!                     half word.
KSUPP   SYN, (8)216242.0
! M IOREJ  221261.00  deck p.135 line 25 carries it.  Lines 6 and 47
!                     are plain branches: half word only.
MIOREJ  SYN, (8)221261.0
! K C BUSY 221065.00  the volume prints the LABEL ROW on another
!                     page: OCR line 9290, "21* 221065.00 ... 0D
!                     K C BUSY  V-I,...".  Line 9's branch-on-bit A
!                     field pins the half word.
KCBUSY  SYN, (8)221065.0
! K ILEGL  220064.00  deck p.127 carries it as a label row from
!                     printed p.160.  Line 18's BXH pins the half
!                     word.
KILEGL  SYN, (8)220064.0
! K K LOC  220065.40  the volume prints the LABEL ROW on another
!                     page: OCR line 8290, "11* 220065.40
!                     221144.63 01  K K LOC ...".  Line 23 pins the
!                     half word -- and note it is the ODD one: the
!                     printed ".74" has bit 50 set, which is the
!                     address's own low bit.
KKLOC   SYN, (8)220065.40
! Z STEVL  221144.40  eight other pages print "B,Z STEVL" against
!                     "221144.50 00" (OCR 7856, 7862, 7969, 8149,
!                     8373, 8470 ...), and a plain B prints
!                     target+.10, so Z STEVL is the ODD half word of
!                     221144.  Lines 24 and 27 here print the two
!                     forms -- a branch-on-bit (".74") and a plain
!                     branch (".50") -- and both carry the same low
!                     address bit, which is what makes this page's
!                     own reading of ".74" as "odd half word"
!                     self-checking rather than assumed.
ZSTEVL  SYN, (8)221144.40
! K G LOCR 220105.40  the volume prints the LABEL ROW on another
!                     page: OCR line 8313, "34* 220105.40
!                     220105.50 00  K G LOCR  B,$  -RETURN".  Line
!                     25 is a SIC, which prints the BARE 24-bit
!                     address, so this row pins K G LOCR TO THE BIT.
KGLOCR  SYN, (8)220105.40
! K G LOC  220100.40  another page prints "B,K G LOC" against
!                     "220100.50 00" (OCR line 8246), and OCR 8306
!                     shows a different label, K POSLD, occupying
!                     220100.00 -- so K G LOC really is the odd half
!                     word.  Line 26 pins the half word.
KGLOC   SYN, (8)220100.40
! K SEOP T 221350.40  two other pages print "B,K SEOP T" against
!                     "221350.50 00" (OCR 8022, 8259).  Line 45 is
!                     an LVI and pins only the half word.
KSEOPT  SYN, (8)221350.40
! K EOP R  221354.40  the volume prints the LABEL ROW on another
!                     page: OCR line 9654, "12* 221354.40
!                     221354.74 C2  K EOP R  BRZ,$  -IF NONE ON,
!                     ALLS WELL", and four other pages print
!                     "SIC,K EOP R" against "221354.40 80".  Line
!                     46 is a SIC, so it pins K EOP R TO THE BIT.
KEOPR   SYN, (8)221354.40

! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ORIGIN.  The page prints NO SLC.  217573.00 is line 1's
! printed LOCATION and 217573 is the heading row's own "LOCATION"
! field; see THE JOINS for the three witnesses that never touch
! this page's ink.  Stated hard so the deck assembles standalone,
! which ASSERTS it rather than checking it.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)217573.0

! ---- tail of the disk request path -------------------------------
        CM1111(BU,1), SRD($11)       ! line  1
        KVI,4,.32                    ! line  2
        BZXE,$+1.32                  ! line  3  (same card as 2)
        CM1111(BU,1), SHSECH         ! line  4
        LVI,1,KSUPP                  ! line  5
        B,MIOREJ                     ! line  6  (same card as 5)
ZDISK1  BB,SLOCSU,$+2.0              ! line  7
        BZB,SHSECH,$+2.0             ! line  8
        BBZ,SCHOP($10),KCBUSY        ! line  9
        L(BU,9-SDD),SCW.28($13),-18  ! line 10  -CW COUNT OFFSET BY 9 FOR ARCS
        LV,8,$L                      ! line 11
        CT0111(BU,9),SCW+.37($13),-27 ! line 12 -TEST IF COMPLETE ARC
        BZRZ,$+1.0                   ! line 14  -BRANCH IF NOT COMPLETE ARC
        V-I,8,1.0                    ! line 15  -SUBTRACT 1 FOR FULL ARC REQUEST
        V+,8,SCUARC($13)             ! line 16  -ADD CURRENT ARC ADDRESS
        KV,8,SMXARC($13)             ! line 17  -COMPARE WITH MAX ARC ADDR
        BXH,KILEGL                   ! line 18  -TOO MANY ARCS
        LV,0,SCUARC($13)             ! line 19
        L(BU,18),SARCAD($10),-18     ! line 20
        KV,0,$L                      ! line 21  -TEST IF LOC NEEDED
        BXE,$+1.32                   ! line 22
        BZB1,SLOCSU,KKLOC            ! line 23
        BZBZ,SSEL($11),ZSTEVL        ! line 24
        SIC,KGLOCR                   ! line 25
        B,KGLOC                      ! line 26  (same card as 25)
        B,ZSTEVL                     ! line 27

! ---- *****CHANGE MODE***** ---------------------------------------
KMODE   SV,1,$L                      ! line 35
        KF(BU,2),SCMODE($10),-18     ! line 36  -IS CHAN SET TO DESIRED MODE
        BAE,KKMODE                   ! line 37  -IF SO, RETURN
        LV,1,KMCODE-1.0($1)          ! line 38  -IF NOT, PICK UP OCTAL CODE
        SVA,1,KMODEC.32              ! line 39
        SF(BU,2),SCMODE($10),-18     ! line 40  -RESET CHAN ST TABLE
        CNOP                         ! line 41  (emits nothing: already aligned)
        LX,0,$+1.                    ! line 42
        SX,0,SIOINS($12)             ! line 43  (same card as 42)
KMODEC  CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),0.0       ! line 44  -MODE CHANGE COMMAND
        LVI,1,KSEOPT                 ! line 45
        SIC,KEOPR                    ! line 46
        B,MIOREJ                     ! line 47  (same card as 46) -WAIT FOR END OP
KKMODE  B,$                          ! line 48
KMCODE  XW,(8)57.,                   ! line 49  -ECC ODD PARITY
        XW,(8)156.,                  ! line 50  -NO-ECC EVEN PARITY
        XW,(8)157.,                  ! line 51  -NO-ECC ODD PARITY

